# Code Style

## No nested or packed ternaries

Never nest a ternary inside another ternary. When flattening multi-branch conditionals,
convert the **entire** decision to `if` / `else` / early returns — do not leave sibling
ternaries packed into the same statement (array literal, argument list, object literal,
or template).

`??` / `?.` chains are fine. A single trivial ternary on its own line is fine.

```ts
// ❌ Nested ternary
const title = editing
  ? "Finish editing first"
  : scanning
    ? "Wait for scan"
    : "Import clip";

// ❌ Half-fix — sibling ternaries still packed in one statement
if (rounded > 0 || border > 0) {
  jsarguments = [rounded > 0 ? rounded : 6, border > 0 ? 1 : 0];
}
emit("ui", "transforms", invert ? 1 : 0, reverse ? 1 : 0);

// ✅ Flat control flow
let title = "Import clip";
if (editing) title = "Finish editing first";
else if (scanning) title = "Wait for scan";

let cornerRadius = 6;
if (rounded > 0) cornerRadius = rounded;
let drawBorder = 0;
if (border > 0) drawBorder = 1;
jsarguments = [cornerRadius, drawBorder];

emit("ui", "transforms", Number(invert), Number(reverse));
```

## No trampoline functions

A function that does nothing but call another function is a trampoline — delete it and inline
the implementation at the call site(s).

```ts
// ❌ Trampoline — resolveVelocity just calls resolveVelocityCurved
function resolveVelocityCurved(note, curved) { ... }
export function resolveVelocity(note, motif, v) {
  return resolveVelocityCurved(note, applyVelocityCurve(v, motif.curve));
}

// ✅ Implementation lives in the exported function; hot-path inlines the body
export function resolveVelocity(note, motif, v) {
  const curved = applyVelocityCurve(v, motif.curve);
  const base = note.velocity ?? curved;
  return Math.round(clamp(base * (note.velocityScale ?? 1) + (note.velocityOffset ?? 0), 1, 127));
}
// Hot path that pre-computes: inline the 3-line body directly
```

## No re-exports

Import from the defining module, not through a middleman. This covers value re-exports
**and** type aliases that only rename an imported type — both invent a second name for
the same symbol and hide the source of truth.

```ts
// ❌ motif-authoring.ts re-exports from note-edit-schema.ts
export { NOTE_EDIT_FIELDS, type NoteEditField } from "./note-edit-schema.js";
// authoring-controller.ts imports via the middleman
import { type NoteEditField } from "../library/motif-authoring.js";

// ❌ Type rename alias (same smell: second name, no new shape)
import type { MotifPreviewPaintData } from "../core/preview.js";
export type LibraryMotifPreviewData = MotifPreviewPaintData;
preview: LibraryMotifPreviewData;

// ✅ Import from the source directly; use the defining name at the use site
import type { NoteEditField } from "../library/note-edit-schema.js";
import type { MotifPreviewPaintData } from "../core/preview.js";
preview: MotifPreviewPaintData;
```

Allowed: a new `interface` / `type` that adds or narrows fields. Not allowed: a pure
`export type Foo = Bar` (or `type Foo = Bar` used only to rebrand `Bar`).
